  "result": "Implemented `netgraph/graph.py` and all 3 tests in `tests/test_t1.py` pass under `python -m pytest -q` using the repo's Python 3.12 venv.\n\nI chose **Approach A, adjacency sets**. A per-node `set[int]` gives O(1) edge lookup and duplicate detection while keeping memory proportional to nodes plus edges, which suits the sparse graphs the BFS shortest-path API implies. The matrix would cost n\u00b2 memory with no benefit for this interface.\n\nDetails of the implementation:\n\n- **Edges** are stored symmetrically in both endpoints' sets, with a running edge counter incremented only on first insertion, so duplicates are no-ops and self-loops count once.\n- **Neighbors** returns a sorted list, as the spec requires.\n- **Shortest path** is a BFS that expands neighbors in ascending order and reconstructs the path via a parent map, returning `[u]` for `u == v` and `None` when unreachable.\n- **Range checks** raise `IndexError` on every method that takes a node id, covering negative ids too.\n\nNothing is committed. The only new tracked-candidate file is `netgraph/graph.py`, plus `__pycache__` directories from the test run.",
